//
// loft#1221's second REFUSAL cell, in its own file because the first one's check fires in pass
// 1 and a pass-1 error stops the file before this pass-2 check runs. The siblings are
// `1221-an-admissible-source-reaches-a-route.loft` (positive) and
// `1221b-an-unroutable-source-is-refused.loft` (the bare-variant ambiguity).
//
// @falsified-at: e695a6f5 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   The direction inverts for an `@EXPECT_ERROR` file: the corpus runner scores it by whether
//   its expectation MATCHES, so the parent — where nothing fires — is the run that exits 1. The
//   cell COMPILES and runs there, so a naive exit reading of the PROGRAM would call it clean:
//   it writes nothing, `len` reads 0, and there is no diagnostic.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees,
//   measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against e695a6f5; falsify prints only the
//   channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both. The
//   exit/expectations channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not
//   scored in that run.

struct E1221c { k: integer, n: integer }
struct D1221c { h: hash<E1221c[k]> }

// A keyed collection appended to another of the same type. `(Col-Insert)` is stated over records
// joining a collection and says nothing about merging two, so whether this should WORK is a
// design question — but it silently emitted no write, and a drop is not an answer to a design
// question. Refused until the rules say otherwise.
//
// The VECTOR twin is what keeps this scoped: there the whole-collection source IS
// concatenation, it works, and `1215b-an-admissible-append-source-still-works.loft` asserts it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot append a whole `hash<E1221c[k]>` to another
fn test_1221c_a_whole_keyed_collection_has_no_append_route() {
  o1221c: hash<E1221c[k]> = [];
  o1221c += E1221c { k: 1, n: 9 };
  d1221c = D1221c { h: [] };
  d1221c.h += o1221c;
  assert(len(d1221c.h) == 1, "unreached");
}

// The LOCAL spelling of the same statement, and it is the cell that keeps the refusal from
// being narrowed back to the FIELD.
//
// The two place kinds fail DIFFERENTLY, which is why neither alone settles the question and why
// this file needs both. A keyed field emits no write and `len` reads 0. Between two locals the
// statement IS claimed, and what it does is REBIND — the IR is a plain `b = a`, so the
// destination is repointed at the source's store. From an EMPTY destination that is
// indistinguishable from a successful merge (`b=1 a=1`), which is exactly how it was read on the
// way to this file; the two cells that separate it are a POPULATED destination, where the
// destination's own records are simply gone (`d[1] = …; d += c` leaves `d[1]` ABSENT and the
// source's `d[9]` present), and mutating the SOURCE afterwards, where the destination follows.
// Its own `= []` store is orphaned with it — `1 stores not freed at program exit`.
//
// So the local was never a merge to preserve, and a build that refuses only the field has been
// told otherwise by the empty-destination appearance. No `.loft` file in the tree merges two
// keyed locals, so a whole-corpus differential runs clean over the difference — this cell is
// the only thing that reports it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot append a whole `hash<E1221c[k]>` to another
fn test_1221c_two_keyed_locals_are_refused_too() {
  m1221c: hash<E1221c[k]> = [];
  m1221c[7] = E1221c { k: 7, n: 77 };
  p1221c: hash<E1221c[k]> = [];
  p1221c += m1221c;
  assert(len(p1221c) == 1, "unreached");
}
